## Use clear language

Use active verbs: **Turn on**, **Turn off**, **Set temperature**, **Save changes**, **Try again**. Avoid “execute”, “initialise”, “entity service invocation” and similar implementation language in household controls.

Name the object where ambiguity matters: **Turn off kitchen lights** is clearer than **Off** in a group of controls. Use sentence case. Address the user as “you” when useful. Avoid negative contractions in instructions.

These rules follow [GOV.UK clear language](https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/) and [tone guidance](https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/right-tone/).

## Distinguish state from action

| Situation | State label | Action label |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Light is off | Off | Turn on |
| Heating is requesting heat | Heating | Change temperature |
| A command is awaiting confirmation | Turning on… | Keep the pending action disabled only while needed |
| No current reading is available | Unavailable | Try again, if an actual retry is possible |
| There are no current alerts | No current alerts | View history |

Do not label missing information as zero. Do not report success before the device or service confirms the result. Use the user's configured entity names; do not silently rename their devices to satisfy a writing rule.

## Explain errors

Say what happened and what the user can do. For example: “The kitchen light did not respond. Check that it has power, then try again.” Keep raw error details behind a diagnostic disclosure when they help troubleshooting.

Do not invent a recovery action. If the interface cannot retry, explain where the user can check the device.

## Structure information

Put important information first. Keep headings descriptive and short. Use lists for parallel choices and numbered steps for a sequence. Avoid repeated introductions and generic headings. [Clear structure guidance](https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-structure/)

## Links and units

Link text describes its destination or task. Use “View energy use”, not “Click here”. Keep numbers with their units, use locale-aware formatting and show the relevant time period. Do not hardcode pounds, Celsius or a UK timezone over a user's configured preferences.

For English documentation use UK spelling. Preserve native Home Assistant translations in the application rather than replacing every label with hardcoded English.
